### Comparison to paper

| Method | Paper @5% | Paper @7.5% | Paper @10% | Ours @5% | Ours @7.5% | Ours @10% |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| FBP | 18.37 | 16.16 | 14.32 | 14.56 | 13.65 | 13.22 |
| TV | 21.47 | 19.17 | 16.95 | 19.67 | 18.35 | 17.50 |
| TFPnP | 24.50 | 24.23 | 23.72 | **27.27** | **26.58** | **26.10** |

Three observations:

1. **Our FBP and TV are 3–4 dB lower than the paper's.** Reason: harder geometry (512×512, 30 views, ~724 detectors vs paper's 128×128, 30 views, 182 detectors). More pixels × angle in the same number of views.
2. **Our TFPnP is 3 dB higher than the paper's**, despite the harder geometry. Reason: **in-domain training** (LIDC CT) vs paper's **cross-domain** (PASCAL VOC). When the training data matches the test data, the policy learns CT-specific σ/µ schedules.
3. **The TFPnP-to-Fixed-PnP gap widens with noise** (we go +2.96 → +4.16 → +4.61 dB across 5%, 7.5%, 10%). Direct empirical support for the paper's "tuning-free" claim: knowing how to adjust σ/µ adaptively matters more at higher noise.

### Adding HaarPSI

The paper used PSNR only. We added SSIM (structural quality) and HaarPSI (perceptual quality, via the `piq` library) because PSNR can be misleading for medical reconstructions — smooth reconstructions get high PSNR but lose perceptually-meaningful detail.

**Finding:** TFPnP wins on HaarPSI at all noise levels, **but the HaarPSI gap to Fixed PnP is smaller than the PSNR gap** (Δ = 0.080 vs 2.96 dB at 5%). This suggests TFPnP's reconstruction is smoother than Fixed PnP's, which helps PSNR more than HaarPSI. Worth raising as a discussion point: **is the PSNR-based reward optimising the right metric?** Run_07 (SSIM reward) directly tests this.

